#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61861939-69d4-5b1e-a0f0-6a1b383b775b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "31dd152b-cab9-5754-9335-42ed830d3c01" :question/math-academy-id "e-5343" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/52/527ab20a54cc8191b5ef618d99601e77ca2ef4e757ee5f83ddf4c868bf0e5b15.png)" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a right-opening parabola is the left-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/bf/bfb2b1ce3cccea8a22732a061dd8ace5ad1b3c808feb5c1ec074802f7daf4d1a.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-4,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d27b85e4-ddae-5b5f-965a-5dff51b8449b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "34e8e4aa-b8c8-518d-857a-42ac8f7345b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7472" :question/problem "What is the vertex of the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/41/4183a3f0f864d0bda19d9b4c42d69952af51423fb1024d311b6a863e8e4ee037.png)" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a downward-opening parabola is the highest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/fb/fb7fa7a4dcc4c7746122cd01a79a03584eb74c2ce9439859910699278e971a56.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(4,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67455965-c9d3-5ae8-8b56-2a58af95df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "40e08a6f-a312-5f00-8b4a-e5fe8fe3cefb" :question/math-academy-id "e-7475" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+1)^2=8(y-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4(2)(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p =2.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h= -1$ and $k=2.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e302810-8b14-5c8c-8bbb-245386a66b7c" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "258f64db-05c1-5314-a6d8-aa2f604f5c95" :question/math-academy-id "e-7476" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-2)^2=2(x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{2}{4}\\right)(x +1),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)(x +1),\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-1$ and $k=2.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $\\left(-1,2\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d27b85e4-ddae-5b5f-965a-5dff51b8449b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5893672c-56fb-5412-997c-3a5a3164163a" :answer/type {: